      }
    ],
    "sections": [
      {
        "pages": {
          "end": 2,
          "start": 1
        },
        "source_span": {
          "end_page": 2,
          "end_text": "the normal phase in the infrared limit is described by the inverse Green’s function of the SYK model at small p, whereas it crosses over to free fermions for large p values.",
          "start_page": 1,
          "start_text": "The Planckian relaxation rate ~/tP = 2πkBT sets a characteristic timescale for both the equilibration of quantum critical systems and maximal quantum chaos."
        },
        "summary": "Introduces Planckian relaxation and maximal chaos via SYK, then defines a (0+1)-dimensional superconducting quantum dot coupled to the complex SYK model. It gives the Hamiltonian and the large-M,N saddle-point equations, then describes the normal-phase crossover from an SYK non-Fermi liquid at small p to free fermions at large p.",
        "title": "Planckian superconductor model and normal phase"
      },
      {
        "pages": {
          "end": 3,
          "start": 2
        },
        "source_span": {
          "end_page": 3,
          "end_text": "there is only a single critical temperature as the normal phase is always set by the free fermions.",
          "start_page": 2,
          "start_text": "The gap equation (9) at ∆ = 0 makes a boundary in between the normal phase and the superconducting one by setting the critical temperature Tc as a function of the coupling rate t."
        },
        "summary": "Explains the competition between SYK non-Fermi liquid, free fermions, and superconducting condensate. It defines the zero-temperature critical coupling and shows that for N>2M the critical temperature bends, producing an upper and lower critical temperature; p=1/2 separates reentrant from single-Tc behavior, and a random free-fermion model lacks reentrance.",
        "title": "Reentrant superconductivity and critical temperatures"
      },
      {
        "pages": {
          "end": 5,
          "start": 3
        },
        "source_span": {
          "end_page": 5,
          "end_text": "same timescale serves as an ultimate bound on manybody quantum chaos,⁸ saturated in strongly coupled systems without quasiparticle excitations.",
          "start_page": 3,
          "start_text": "From Figure 2 (right), one notices the pairing gap at the critical coupling is ∝ T at low temperatures."
        },
        "summary": "Presents the central result: at the critical coupling the pairing gap grows linearly with T and saturates ∆≈2πT, i.e. η~/tP with η order one. It compares the phenomenon to Andreev-billiard gaps, derives ∆∝T from the low-temperature gap equation, estimates ∆(T)≃√(6π)T (η≈1.22) versus numerical η≈0.96, and concludes that the gap is set by the Planckian time and may characterize cSYK quantum dots.",
        "title": "Planckian pairing gap at the critical coupling"
      },
      {
        "pages": {
          "end": 7,
          "start": 5
        },
        "source_span": {
          "end_page": 7,
          "end_text": "The critical coupling tc is found as a condition when the off-set δ of the interpolating function ∆ = 2πη T + δ vanishes",
